WEBVTT

00:00.600 --> 00:05.413
A hovercraft floats above land, water, mud or ice on a cushion of air.

00:05.413 --> 00:10.845
Invented in Britain in the 1950s, it reshaped how we imagined travel across any

00:10.845 --> 00:11.395
terrain.

00:12.295 --> 00:16.817
A fan drives air beneath the hull. A flexible skirt traps that cushion, lifting the

00:16.817 --> 00:19.377
craft, while separate thrust pushes it forward.

00:20.277 --> 00:25.998
British engineer Christopher Cockerell built the first working model in Somerleyton.

00:25.998 --> 00:31.241
His SR.N1 crossed the English Channel in 1959, and a later flexible skirt let

00:31.241 --> 00:34.101
hovercraft glide over waves and obstacles.

00:35.001 --> 00:38.195
The SR.N4 turned this idea into big business.

00:38.195 --> 00:43.943
Entering service in 1968, it carried two hundred fifty four passengers and thirty

00:43.943 --> 00:45.647
cars across the Channel.

00:46.547 --> 00:51.569
Operated by Hoverlloyd and Seaspeed, it ran like an airline, with flight numbers and

00:51.569 --> 00:56.531
thirty minute crossings, before retiring in 2000 after thirty two years of service.

00:57.431 --> 01:02.487
Rising fuel costs and the Channel Tunnel ended the big ferries, but hovercraft live

01:02.487 --> 01:07.361
on, in rescue, military, and sport, a lasting testament to a bold idea that once

01:07.361 --> 01:09.249
redefined cross-Channel travel.
